d. observations, the so called Big Data regime, ()/p0()NY i=1p (yij): (4) Here, both computing the gradient term rlog(t) and evaluating the Metropolis-Hastings acceptance ratio require a computational budget that scales unfeasibly as O(N). One approach is to use a standard random walk proposal instead of Langevin dynamics, and to eciently approximating the Metropolis-Hastings accept-reject mechanism using only a subset of the data (Korattikara et al., 2014; Bardenet et al., 2014). This paper is concerned with stochastic gradient Langevin dynamics (SGLD), an alter- native approach proposed by Welling and Teh (2011). This follows the opposite route and chooses to completely avoid the computation of the Metropolis-Hastings ratio. By choos- ing a discretization of the Langevin diusion (1) with a suciently small step-size 1, because the Langevin diusion is ergodic with respect to , the hope is that even if the Metropolis-Hastings accept-reject mechanism is completely avoided, the resulting Markov chain still has an invariant distribution that is close to . Choosing a decreasing sequence of step-sizes m!0 should even allow us to converge to the exact posterior distribution. To further make this approach viable in large Nsettings, the gradient term rlog() can be further approximated using a subsampling strategy. For an integer 1 nNand a random subset def= (1;:::;n) of [N]f1;:::;Nggenerated by sampling with or without replacement from [ N], the quantity rlog p0() +N nnX i=1rlog p(xij) (5) is an unbiased estimator of rlog(). Most importantly, this stochastic estimate can be computed with a computational budget that scales as O(n) withnpotentially much smaller thanN. Indeed, the larger the quotient n=N, the smaller the variance of this estimate. Stochastic gradient methods have a long history in optimisation and machine learning and are especially relevant in the large dataset regime considered in this article (Robbins 4 Consistency and Fluctuation