can use instead discretizations of an Hamiltonian system of ordinary dierential equations (Duane et al., 1987; Neal, 2010); when coupled with the stochastic estimates to the gradient above described, this leads to the stochastic gradient Hamiltonian Monte Carlo algorithm of (Chen et al., 2014). In the rest of this paper, we will build a rigorous framework for understanding the prop- erties of this SGLD algorithm, demonstrating that the heuristics and numerical evidences presented in Welling and Teh (2011) were indeed correct. 3. Assumptions and Stability Analysis This section starts with the basics assumptions we will need for the asymptotic results to follow, and illustrates some of the potential stability issues that may occur, would the SGLD algorithm be applied without care. 3.1 Basic Assumptions Throughout this text, we assume that the sequence of step-sizes = (m)m1satises the following usual assumption. Assumption 1 The step-sizes = (m)m1form a decreasing sequence with lim m!1m= 0 and lim m!1Tm=1: Indeed, this assumption is easily seen to also be necessary for the Law of Large Numbers of Section 4 to hold. Furthermore, we will need at several occasions to assume the following assumption on the oscillations of a sequence of step-sizes ( !m)m1. Assumption 2 The step-sizes sequence (!m)m1is such that !m!0and m!1 and lim m!1X m1(!m=m)= m<1 andX m1!2 m=[m 2 m]<1: where (!m=m)def=!m+1=m+1␀!m=m. 6 Consistency and Fluctuations for Stochastic Gradient Langevin Dynamics Remark 3 Assumption 2 holds if = (m)m1satises Assumption (1)and the weights are dened as !m=p m, for some some exponent p1small enough for  m!1 . This is because the rst sum is less thanP m1(!m=m)= 1=p␀1 1= 1, while the niteness of the second sum can be seen as follows: X m1!2 m=␀ m 2 m .1 +X m2(!m=m)2(1= m␀1␀1= m) .1 +X m2(1= m␀1␀1= m) = 1 + 1= 1: For any exponents 0<  < 1and0< p < 1=the sequences m= (m0+m)␀and !m=p msatisfy both Assumption 1 and Assum