pectations. For more exhaustive simulation studies we refer to Welling and Teh (2011); S. Ahn and Welling (2012); Patterson and Teh (2013a); Chen et al. (2014). By considering a logistic regression model, we demonstrate that the SGLD can be advantageous over the Metropolis- Adjusted-Langevin (MALA) algorithm if the available computational budget only allows a few iterations through the whole data set, see Section 7.2.2. 20 Consistency and Fluctuations for Stochastic Gradient Langevin Dynamics 7.1 Linear Gaussian model ConsiderNindependent and identically distributed observations ( xi)N i=1from the two pa- rameters location model given by xijN(;2 x): We use a Gaussian prior N(0;2 ) and assume that the variance hyper-parameters 2  and2 xare both known. The posterior density () is normally distributed with mean p and variance 2 pgiven by p= x 1 +2 x N2 ␀1 and2 p=2 x N 1 +2 x N2 ␀1 where x= (x1+:::+xN)=Nis the sample average of the observations. In this case, we have rlog() =␀␀p 2pandH(;U) =n (N=n)X j2In(U)xj␀X 1iNxio =2 x for a random subset In(U)[N] of cardinal n. 7.1.1 Verification of Assumption 4 We verify in this section that Assumption (4) is satised for the following choice of Lyapunov function, V() = 1 +(␀p)2 22p: Since the error term H(;U) is globally bounded, the drift (1 =2)rlogand the Lyapunov functionVare linear, Assumptions (4).1 and (4).2 are satised. Finally, to verify Assump- tion (4).3, it suces to note that since rlog() =␀(␀p)=2 pwe have  rV();1 2rlog() =␀(␀p)2 24p=1␀V() 2p: In other words, Assumption (4).3 holds with == 1=2 p. 7.1.2 Simulations We chose= 1,x= 5 and created a data set consisting of N= 100 data points simulated from the model. We used n= 10 as the size of subsets used to estimate the gradients. We evaluated the convergence behaviour of SGLD using the test function A' where'= sin (x␀p␀0:5p). We are interested in conrming the asymptotic convergence regimes of Theore